      "content": "No single moral framework universally justifies preemptive strikes since the legitimacy of such actions depends entirely on the specific context, including the immediacy of the threat and the proportionality of the response. Within a strict liberal democratic framework, intervention is generally only considered moral if it strictly adheres to the W Hastings Doctrine and the UN Charter, which prioritizes peaceful resolution and requires an imminent threat of mass atrocities. Therefore, while preventing authoritarian aggression can be a Noble Cause, it becomes unjustifiable if the military response is disproportionate, requires excessive civilian casualties, or bypasses authorized international diplomatic channels.",
